{"name":"Sounds of Nature","hex":"#dfe5d7","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sounds","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of sound"]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["third-person singular simple present indicative of sound"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sounds"},{"term":"nature","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The way things are, the totality of all things in the physical universe and their order, especially the physical world in contrast to spiritual realms and flora and fauna as distinct from human conventions, art, and technology.","The particular way someone or something is, especially\nThe essential or innate characteristics of a person or thing which will always tend to manifest, especially in contrast to specific contexts, reason, religious duty, upbringing, and personal pretense or effort."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To endow with natural qualities."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/nature"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sounds of Nature","blocks":[{"text":"Sounds of Nature ( 天籁 ) is a work for unconventional Instruments ,\ncomposed by He Xuntian in 1986."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/05/He_Xuntian_works.png/330px-He_Xuntian_works.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sounds_of_Nature","wikidata":"Q28187198"},{"title":"Sound","blocks":[{"text":"Sound is a phenomenon in which pressure disturbances propagate through an elastic material medium. In the context of physics, it is characterised as a mechanical wave of pressure or related quantities, whereas in physiological-psychological contexts it implies a hearing process. Though sensitivity to sound varies among all organisms, the human ear is sensitive to audio frequencies ranging from 20 Hz to 20 kHz. More broadly, acoustic waves encompass mechanical longitudinal waves in general.\nExamples of the significance and application of sound include music, medical imaging techniques, oral language and parts of science."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/Thoth08BigasDrumEvansChalmette.jpg/330px-Thoth08BigasDrumEvansChalmette.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sound","wikidata":"Q11461"},{"title":"Nature","blocks":[{"text":"Nature, in the broadest sense, is the physical world or universe. In this general sense nature refers to the laws, elements and phenomena of the physical world, including life. Although humans are part of nature, human activity or humans as a whole are often described as at times at odds, or outright separate and even superior to nature."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Startrails_above_Gunung_Bromo_-_Indonesia.jpg/330px-Startrails_above_Gunung_Bromo_-_Indonesia.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nature","wikidata":"Q7860"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/MIT_4.401_Introduction_to_Building_Technology%2C_Spring_2006_-_Sound_source%2C_Wavelength%2C_and_Amplitude_-_3043793636.jpg/500px-MIT_4.401_Introduction_to_Building_Technology%2C_Spring_2006_-_Sound_source%2C_Wavelength%2C_and_Amplitude_-_3043793636.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/MIT_4.401_Introduction_to_Building_Technology%2C_Spring_2006_-_Sound_source%2C_Wavelength%2C_and_Amplitude_-_3043793636.jpg/500px-MIT_4.401_Introduction_to_Building_Technology%2C_Spring_2006_-_Sound_source%2C_Wavelength%2C_and_Amplitude_-_3043793636.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Diagram of sound source, wavelength, and amplitude.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMIT_4.401_Introduction_to_Building_Technology